{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_09c87e63b81a3516900398b5c5f30996f45fc788d5a542a3b8a57eb99a9e94f6"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: setup instructions are provided for Windows (via WSL2), Linux Debian/Ubuntu, macOS Intel, and macOS Apple Silicon."},{"claim_ids":["clm_13a9d0df56a7f36ec11182e0983c6e24b0b623b4c0d51248bda1a97a5b7d1a9a"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The evidence consists solely of README documentation; no source code, tests, or runtime configuration appear in the provided slices, so no product runtime behavior can be verified from this snapshot."},{"claim_ids":["clm_19cc1cda2e7f4cf982c258198c9bf303d6c68d5cfb014b70729964c78ceb017f"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: uv is presented as the Python package and virtual-environment manager, with uv run allowing scripts to execute without manual venv activation."},{"claim_ids":["clm_48f322b4402d06a4c0ae1eebb4d03aa41640f6627b1d63c1472c3409e0d99ab4"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: the README is a tutorial instructing learners to set up an interactive development environment aligned with AI Makerspace best practices, covering terminal, CLI, git, uv, and pip usage."},{"claim_ids":["clm_559190610ad37df103b5d585981cdba818efabbf9c5d6fd93299abd9df71294e"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: the guide includes SSH key setup for GitHub (ssh-keygen, adding the public key in GitHub settings) and instructions for creating, cloning, and branching a personal repo."},{"claim_ids":["clm_64b037bff0c7b1a924f624ed146daf1b5e32e335c4b139a64ba485959a1d772c"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The repository is an educational onboarding tutorial for AI engineers rather than a shipped software product; its content is environment-setup guidance for building, shipping, and sharing LLM applications."},{"claim_ids":["clm_7a0caec4689f8214c60805923b3ce793d3541b2643c7e1c5226965deb10bef2f"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: learners add the AI Makerspace Beyond-ChatGPT repository as an extra git remote named BC, fetch it, track it in a BCBranch, and merge it into main with --allow-unrelated-histories."},{"claim_ids":["clm_be671c74617d7851684287c17a508830ff4f2e63fec6cea39e694d90fcb516dd"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: editor configuration steps cover installing the Python and Jupyter extensions in Cursor/VS Code and selecting the uv-created Python interpreter."},{"claim_ids":["clm_e0a8b23149940bae338aebb719a336a1446c181a590d474a8cd3590e29de2014"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: learners are walked through generating GitHub classic access tokens (90-day expiration recommended), OpenAI API keys, and Claude/Anthropic API keys for use in course projects."},{"claim_ids":["clm_f49023cc2aa28943068f6f3e355c19e10f5578dbd59a499f51e2037c07ddc5c4"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Repository development practice: the tutorial recommends Cursor as the best-practice AI editor, also supports VS Code, and notes Cursor is a fork of VS Code like Windsurf."}],"claim_ids":["clm_09c87e63b81a3516900398b5c5f30996f45fc788d5a542a3b8a57eb99a9e94f6","clm_13a9d0df56a7f36ec11182e0983c6e24b0b623b4c0d51248bda1a97a5b7d1a9a","clm_19cc1cda2e7f4cf982c258198c9bf303d6c68d5cfb014b70729964c78ceb017f","clm_48f322b4402d06a4c0ae1eebb4d03aa41640f6627b1d63c1472c3409e0d99ab4","clm_559190610ad37df103b5d585981cdba818efabbf9c5d6fd93299abd9df71294e","clm_64b037bff0c7b1a924f624ed146daf1b5e32e335c4b139a64ba485959a1d772c","clm_7a0caec4689f8214c60805923b3ce793d3541b2643c7e1c5226965deb10bef2f","clm_be671c74617d7851684287c17a508830ff4f2e63fec6cea39e694d90fcb516dd","clm_e0a8b23149940bae338aebb719a336a1446c181a590d474a8cd3590e29de2014","clm_f49023cc2aa28943068f6f3e355c19e10f5578dbd59a499f51e2037c07ddc5c4"],"created_at":"2026-09-14T04:41:57Z","created_by_operation":"op_dabef326020e46de8c9cff4ca4f939c8","id":"p